  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Tuesday/... \nStrong high pressure will continue to be the dominant synoptic \nfeature through early next week with H5 heights 596-598dm sprawling \nacross the forecast area. Unsurprisingly given this pattern, \nwidespread major HeatRisk will continue with clear cut warning \ncriteria persisting through the weekend as temperatures hover 5F-10F \nabove normal. The preponderance of ensemble output suggests the \ncenter of the subtropical anti-cylcone slowly lifting through the \nfour corners and into Colorado during the first part of next week \nallowing easterly flow, subtle shortwaves, and stronger, moist \noutflows to return to Arizona. Easterly wave energy will also help \nerode some of the higher midlevel heights and temperatures while \nalso aiding the displacement of the anti-cyclone center. These \ncombined efforts should allow local H5 heights to fall below 594dm \nthough the timing at which this evolution occurs is somewhat \nuncertain. Among GEFS members, there is fairly convincing evidence \nof enhanced convection Sunday allowing deeper outflow intrusions \nwhile H5 heights fall below 594dm reducing or eliminating the \nextreme heat threat Monday. EPS members are more suggestive of \nbetter convective potential delayed until Monday evening, but \nregardless temperatures should return to near normal levels by the \nmiddle of next week with the diurnal thunderstorm risks also \nreturning to the region.",
